1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,703
Ask any grandma for her recipe, and

2
00:00:02,753 --> 00:00:04,387
you'll hear something like this.

3
00:00:04,437 --> 00:00:05,513
A bit of oil.

4
00:00:05,563 --> 00:00:06,697
A pinch of salt.

5
00:00:06,747 --> 00:00:08,589
Enough soya sauce for the colour.

6
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:10,881
Until it smells right.

7
00:00:11,506 --> 00:00:13,604
Our most loved family recipes were never

8
00:00:13,654 --> 00:00:15,009
written down.

9
00:00:15,059 --> 00:00:16,321
They live in a voice, in a

10
00:00:16,371 --> 00:00:18,677
kitchen, in someone's hands.

11
00:00:18,727 --> 00:00:20,047
And when we try to capture them,

12
00:00:20,097 --> 00:00:21,742
we lose the best parts:

13
00:00:21,792 --> 00:00:24,865
the stories, the 'my mother always said',

14
00:00:24,915 --> 00:00:26,189
the little words that make a dish

15
00:00:26,239 --> 00:00:27,106
ours.

16
00:00:27,156 --> 00:00:29,324
Worse, most AI tools will happily invent

17
00:00:29,374 --> 00:00:30,914
the missing numbers.

18
00:00:30,964 --> 00:00:32,760
Two tablespoons of salt?

19
00:00:32,810 --> 00:00:34,537
That's a guess, dressed up as grandma's

20
00:00:34,587 --> 00:00:36,015
recipe.

21
00:00:36,555 --> 00:00:38,734
Meet Grandma's Recipe Story.

22
00:00:38,784 --> 00:00:40,894
Record her talking through one dish, and

23
00:00:40,944 --> 00:00:42,623
it becomes a recipe card.

24
00:00:42,673 --> 00:00:44,110
In her words.

25
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,315
Her voice kept right beside it.

26
00:00:46,365 --> 00:00:49,566
And every unknown left honestly unknown.

27
00:00:49,616 --> 00:00:51,645
Built with Meta's Muse models:

28
00:00:51,695 --> 00:00:54,470
Voice Transcribe, and Muse Spark.

29
00:00:55,009 --> 00:00:56,271
Here's how it works.

30
00:00:56,321 --> 00:00:57,931
Tell us who's cooking, the dish, and

31
00:00:57,981 --> 00:00:59,684
the language she tells it in.

32
00:00:59,734 --> 00:01:01,403
Tick that she's okay with being recorded,

33
00:01:01,453 --> 00:01:03,423
because consent comes first.

34
00:01:03,473 --> 00:01:05,907
Then press record, right in the browser.

35
00:01:05,957 --> 00:01:08,369
One dish, one to five minutes.

36
00:01:08,419 --> 00:01:11,299
Nothing is sent until you save.

37
00:01:11,751 --> 00:01:12,734
Muse

38
00:01:12,784 --> 00:01:15,056
Voice Transcribe turns the recording into

39
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:16,229
text.

40
00:01:16,279 --> 00:01:17,959
For this walkthrough, we're opening a

41
00:01:18,009 --> 00:01:19,538
fictional sample card.

42
00:01:19,588 --> 00:01:22,092
The original transcript is kept unchanged, and

43
00:01:22,142 --> 00:01:24,472
every line gets a stable ID.

44
00:01:24,522 --> 00:01:25,900
The family can fix a name or

45
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:27,897
ingredient, then approve.

46
00:01:27,947 --> 00:01:29,859
Only the approved version is ever sent

47
00:01:29,909 --> 00:01:32,221
for extraction.

48
00:01:32,673 --> 00:01:35,363
Next, Muse Spark builds the recipe.

49
00:01:35,413 --> 00:01:36,803
Look at the ingredients.

50
00:01:36,853 --> 00:01:38,823
Three eggs, two tomatoes:

51
00:01:38,873 --> 00:01:40,030
she said those.

52
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:41,319
But a pinch of salt?

53
00:01:41,369 --> 00:01:42,840
A bit of oil?

54
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:44,454
Spark doesn't guess.

55
00:01:44,504 --> 00:01:46,822
The quantity stays unknown, her exact words

56
00:01:46,872 --> 00:01:48,784
sit right beside it, and every line

57
00:01:48,834 --> 00:01:51,048
links back to its transcript segment.

58
00:01:51,098 --> 00:01:53,033
Those references are checked in code, so

59
00:01:53,083 --> 00:01:55,204
nothing ungrounded lands on the card.

60
00:01:55,254 --> 00:01:56,679
The family story is marked as a

61
00:01:56,729 --> 00:01:59,349
paraphrase. Only verified quotes are her real

62
00:01:59,399 --> 00:02:01,083
words.

63
00:02:01,537 --> 00:02:03,832
Then it asks at most three questions,

64
00:02:03,882 --> 00:02:05,852
only about what's missing.

65
00:02:05,902 --> 00:02:08,209
How much salt do you usually add?

66
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:09,893
The family answers:

67
00:02:09,943 --> 00:02:11,936
half a teaspoon, she uses the small

68
00:02:11,986 --> 00:02:13,318
blue spoon.

69
00:02:13,368 --> 00:02:15,536
That answer is saved separately, with its

70
00:02:15,586 --> 00:02:18,380
own timestamp, as family evidence.

71
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:21,960
It never silently rewrites the draft.

72
00:02:22,412 --> 00:02:24,487
Mark the card reviewed, keep her voice

73
00:02:24,537 --> 00:02:26,402
right next to the recipe, and print

74
00:02:26,452 --> 00:02:27,807
it or save a PDF for the

75
00:02:27,857 --> 00:02:29,363
whole family.

76
00:02:29,413 --> 00:02:31,139
Cards live in your own browser, and

77
00:02:31,189 --> 00:02:34,053
saving, reopening or printing never triggers a

78
00:02:34,103 --> 00:02:36,101
paid AI call.

79
00:02:36,554 --> 00:02:37,677
Under the hood:

80
00:02:37,727 --> 00:02:39,290
record in the browser.

81
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:41,856
Muse Voice Transcribe for the audio.

82
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:43,737
A family approval step.

83
00:02:43,787 --> 00:02:47,057
Then Muse Spark for grounded extraction, only

84
00:02:47,107 --> 00:02:49,035
when you ask for it.

85
00:02:49,489 --> 00:02:51,273
Grandma's Recipe Story.

86
00:02:51,323 --> 00:02:53,537
From her voice, to your kitchen table.

87
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:55,418
Try the sample cards today, and record

88
00:02:55,468 --> 00:02:57,605
her recipe while you still can.

